19.0 on CLR-08 and CLR-18), which could superficially suggest a duplicated measurement entering the test twice; however, these rows belong to different collar_id values in different basins, so they are distinct independent units rather than repeated entries of the same unit.","observed_value":"duplicate flux values across distinct collar_ids","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":3,"end_line":3,"quoted_text":"CLR-02,restored,214,17.0"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":25,"end_line":25,"quoted_text":"CLR-24,restored,270,17.0"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":9,"end_line":9,"quoted_text":"CLR-08,restored,332,19.0"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":19,"end_line":19,"quoted_text":"CLR-18,restored,159,19.0"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to demonstrate the issue by (1) checking every collar_id in data/input.csv for duplicates (all 24 are distinct), (2) searching workflow/analysis.py for any operation that could enter a unit more than once into the t-test, such as row duplication, merging, resampling, or per-subunit expansion (none exists, and a uniqueness guard would raise ValueError on any repeated collar_id), (3) checking whether repeated identical flux values in the restored group indicated the same measurement counted twice (they belong to different collars), and (4) recomputing group n, means, and the mean difference from the raw CSV to confirm the test statistics reflect exactly one entry per independent unit (they do: n=12 per group, means 6.00 and 18.00, difference 12.00). No path by which the same independent unit repeatedly enters the row-independent Welch procedure could be constructed from the supplied files.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]},"ttft_ms":40272,"ttft_stream_ms":2670,"time_to_request_ms":16,"type":"result","duration_ms":83373,"uuid":"ac15e2d4-5ab3-418f-8121-4db2e9c5b7c9"}
